  "theory": {
"intro": "A one-to-one function never gives the same output for two different inputs.",
"facts": [
"A function is one-to-one if every horizontal line meets its graph at most once.",
"If $f(a)=f(b)$ can only happen when $a=b$, the function is one-to-one.",
"One counterexample with $a\\ne b$ and $f(a)=f(b)$ disproves one-to-one behaviour.",
"A one-to-one function has an inverse function on its range.",
"Use the whole stated domain, even when only part of its graph is visible. A polynomial without a domain restriction uses all real inputs.",
"The vertical line test checks whether a relation is a function; the horizontal line test checks whether a function is one-to-one."
],
"steps": [
"Use the given domain and apply the horizontal line test, or compare the given outputs.",
"Explain why no output repeats, or give two different inputs with the same output."
